@using System.Globalization
@using UBookIt.Web.Rendering
@model IBookingFormView
@{
    var hasDates = Model.AvailableDates.Count > 0;
    var lengthLabel = BookingDurations.Label(Model.DurationMinutes);
}
@*
    Which dates can actually be booked, so a visitor chooses one knowing it is bookable
    rather than finding out after a page load. The list and the start times below come
    from ONE reading of availability, so they cannot disagree about the same day.

    FILTERED TO THE CHOSEN LENGTH. A date offering only half-hour gaps is not offered to
    somebody who asked for two hours — a list that ignored the length would move the blind
    pick one step later instead of removing it.

    RADIOS IN A FIELDSET, which is the shape `_Times` already uses for "choose one of
    these". One idiom on the page rather than two, and it inherits an accessible pattern
    instead of inventing one.
*@
@if (hasDates)
{
    <fieldset id="@BookingFieldIds.Dates" class="ubookit-dates">
        <legend>Dates with availability for @lengthLabel in the next @Model.WindowDays days</legend>
        @foreach (var (available, index) in Model.AvailableDates.Select((available, index) => (available, index)))
        {
            var id = $"ubookit-date-{index}";
            <div class="ubookit-dates-option">
                <input type="radio"
                       id="@id"
                       name="@BookingKeys.DateQuery"
                       value="@available.Date.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd")"
                       @(available.IsSelected ? "checked" : null) />
                <label for="@id">@available.Date.ToString("dddd d MMMM yyyy", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)</label>
            </div>
        }
    </fieldset>
}
else if (Model.LongestAvailableInWindowMinutes is { } longest)
{
    @* The window has availability, just not this much of it. Saying which length WOULD find
       something turns "nothing" into a choice — and this is the case where the difference
       matters most, because a window empty at two hours may be full of half-hour gaps. *@
    <p class="ubookit-no-dates" id="ubookit-window-unavailable">
        No date in the next @Model.WindowDays days has @lengthLabel available.
        The longest available in that time is @BookingDurations.Label(longest).
        Please choose a shorter length, or a date further ahead.
    </p>
}
else
{
    @* Nothing at any length. A shorter length would not help, so it is not suggested —
       offering a next move that leads nowhere is worse than naming the one that remains. *@
    <p class="ubookit-no-dates">
        No date in the next @Model.WindowDays days has any availability.
        Please choose a date further ahead.
    </p>
}

@if (!Model.SelectedDateIsListed)
{
    @* THE PAGE WOULD OTHERWISE CONTRADICT ITSELF: a list with nothing selected, beside start
       times for a date the list does not contain. Says which date is being shown rather than
       leaving the visitor to reconcile the two. *@
    <p class="ubookit-dates-showing">
        Showing @Model.SelectedDate.ToString("dddd d MMMM yyyy", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture),
        which is not in the list above.
    </p>
}

<div class="ubookit-field">
    @* A DIFFERENT QUESTION, not a second way to ask the same one — and labelled to say so.
       The list answers "when can I come soon?"; this answers "I want this particular date".

       Its own query parameter, because two controls sharing one name submit BOTH values and
       the winner is whatever model binding happens to pick. This one wins when it is filled
       in: typing a date is the more deliberate act, and a visitor who picks from the list and
       then types is correcting themselves. *@
    @* DELIBERATELY NOT REPOPULATED, and this is load-bearing rather than an oversight.

       Adding `value="@Model.SelectedDate..."` here looks like an obvious improvement — the
       control it replaced did exactly that — and it would break the list entirely: the typed
       date wins over the list by design, so a field that kept resubmitting a date the visitor
       chose three renders ago would make every subsequent click on the list do nothing at all.

       Left empty, it submits an empty value, which fails to parse and falls through to the
       list. The visitor's actual choice is not lost — it is stated above, in "Showing …". *@
    <label for="@BookingFieldIds.OtherDate">Or choose another date</label>
    <input type="date"
           id="@BookingFieldIds.OtherDate"
           name="@BookingKeys.OtherDateQuery"
           min="@Model.MinDate.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd")"
           max="@Model.MaxDate.ToString("yyyy-MM-dd")"
           aria-describedby="ubookit-other-date-hint" />
    <span id="ubookit-other-date-hint" class="ubookit-hint">
        For a date beyond the next @Model.WindowDays days. Availability is shown once you choose.
    </span>
</div>
